course: dictionary
lesson: 3994
title: Gamble: betting and taking risks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Gamble» has two main meanings. The first is about money: to bet money on a game, race or event, hoping to win more but risking what you lose. «Gambling means betting money on something you cannot control, like horses, scratchcards or a claw machine.» The clues are money and betting.
explain: The second meaning is about choices. Someone who takes a gamble hopes things turn out well, even though they might not. Read the words around it: «Do not gamble with real money; play for fun or points.» Here the clue is real money, so gamble means betting with money, not just playing a game.
explain: You can check the meaning by swapping in your own idea and reading the sentence again. «A loot box is like gambling: you pay without knowing what you will get.» If you swap in "risking money on a surprise," the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: «Gamble» can be a verb (to gamble) or a noun (a gamble).
fact: The phrase «take a gamble» means take a risk, hoping things turn out well.
fact: «It paid off» means it worked out well in the end.
fact: Gambling games are designed so that, over time, players usually lose more than they win.
fact: Gambling with money is not allowed for children, so play for fun or points instead.
exercise: «Gambling means betting money on something you cannot control, like horses or scratchcards.» What does gamble mean in this sentence? | To sing loudly at a party | To buy a present for a friend | To play a game for points at school | *To bet money on something you cannot control
exercise: Ruby says, «I'll take a gamble and wear my red boots, even though it might rain.» What does take a gamble mean? | Give up and go home | *Take a risk, hoping it turns out well | Make a loud noise | Buy a new pair of boots
exercise: «A loot box is like gambling: you pay without knowing what you will get.» Find and copy the word that tells you the result is unknown. | box | *knowing | pay | loot
test: «Do not gamble with real money; play for fun or points.» What should you do instead? | Hide your money in a box | *Play for fun or points | Borrow money from a friend to bet | Bet real money on a claw machine
test: «It was a big gamble, and it paid off.» What does paid off mean here? | It was paid back to the shop | It was lost forever | *It worked out well | It cost a lot of money
exam: «Shares are not quite gambling, because you own a real piece of a real business.» Why are shares not quite gambling? | Shares are only for grown-up games | *You own a real piece of a real business | Shares are played with dice | Shares are always free to get
